      ":question/id": "7fae9e59-f738-573e-8f7c-2f4aaa7785f7",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x-y}{x}\\,.\n$$\n\nAssume $x\\ne 0$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=\\frac{2x-y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=\\frac{2x}{x}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y}{x}&=\\frac{2x}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(\\frac{1}{x})}_{P(x)}y&=\\underbrace{2}_{Q(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)=2.$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "f639f35e-421a-53ff-b3d6-c6196395de0d",
