    "question": {
      ":question/id": "1800de53-7e37-572b-90e2-b06fe2dc3dac",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+1{y}^{2}(\\cos{(x )})^{2}=(1-(1)y\\cos{(x )})^{2}\\,.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+1{y}^{2}(\\cos{(x )})^{2}&=(1-(1)y\\cos{(x )})^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+1{y}^{2}(\\cos{(x )})^{2}&=1+1{y}^{2}(\\cos{(x )})^{2}-2 y \\cos{(x )} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=1-2 y \\cos{(x )} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(2 \\cos{(x )})}_{P(x)}y&=\\underbrace{1}_{Q(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2 \\cos{(x )}$ and $Q(x)=1.$",
